<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congress of People's Deputies of Russia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1990">1990</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Congress of People's Deputies of the RSFSR</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congress of People's Deputies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was the highest legislative body of the Russian SFSR and then the Russian Federation from 1990 to 1993. Elected in March 1990 in the republic's first competitive elections, it chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-yeltsin">Boris Yeltsin</a> as chairman of its Supreme Soviet and adopted the Declaration of State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> on 12 June 1990. Yeltsin's decree dissolving it in September 1993 led to a violent constitutional crisis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congress-of-peoples-deputies-of-russia.jpg" alt="Congress of People's Deputies of Russia" width="320" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pianist; derivative work by FM-K · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Russian_Federation_(1992-1993).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Congress was created by an amendment to the constitution of the RSFSR on 27 October 1989 and first met on 16 May 1990. It officially had 1,068 deputies, 900 elected from territorial districts in proportion to population and 168 from national-territorial districts, and two thirds had to be present for a quorum. It alone could adopt and amend the constitution, approve the prime minister, call referendums and impeach the president. Between its sessions, held in the Grand Kremlin Palace, a Supreme Soviet of 252 deputies that it elected passed laws, 333 in all.</p><p>At the first session 86% of the deputies were members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a>. Some 24 factions formed, grouped into a Communist bloc and a Democratic Russia bloc, which at first held the majority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Yeltsin twice fell short of the 531 votes needed before he was elected chairman on 29 May 1990 with 535, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> had spoken against him. After a referendum of 17 March 1991 created a presidency, Yeltsin won the election of 12 June 1991 and was sworn in before the Congress on 10 July; Ruslan Khasbulatov became chairman on 29 October. On 1 November 1991 the Congress gave the president extraordinary powers for 13 months, and its revisions of the 1978 constitution recorded the renaming of the republic as the Russian Federation on 25 December 1991.</p><p>In December 1992 it refused to approve Yegor Gaidar as head of government, and in March 1993 it cancelled the president's extraordinary powers. On 28 March 1993, 617 of 1,033 deputies voted to impeach Yeltsin, short of the two thirds required, and in the referendum of 23 April voters expressed support for him and his policies. Against the Reform Coalition and Democratic Centre stood the Russian Unity and Creative Forces blocs, which together opposed many of his policies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Decree No. 1400 of 21 September 1993, which dissolved the Congress without constitutional authority, led the deputies to dismiss Yeltsin and approve Vice-President Alexander Rutskoy as head of state, and the standoff brought street fighting to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. Pro-presidential forces stormed the White House on 4 October 1993. A new constitution was approved by referendum on 12 December 1993, when the Federal Assembly was elected, and in February 1994 that assembly declared an amnesty for the conflict.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congress of People's Deputies of Russia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
